---
title: "The dev server"
description: "One server behind one file watcher: how a document and a project take the same path, why the second server was folded away, and what opening a preview to the LAN requires."
---

The server (`crates/server`) is the long-running process behind `preview`: an
[axum](https://github.com/tokio-rs/axum) HTTP/websocket server, a
[`notify`](https://github.com/notify-rs/notify) file watcher, and the kernel pool
from [the previous chapter](execution.tmd). There is **one** of them,
`serve_site/mod.rs`, and it serves a whole project and a single document alike.
`serve/mod.rs` is not a second server: it is the shared layer underneath — the
HTTP/asset helpers, port binding, the security guards, the watch predicates and the
CLI error helpers that the rest of the crate imports.

## The save → preview loop

```{mermaid}
flowchart LR
  save["save .tmd"] --> watch["notify watcher<br/>(debounced, filtered)"]
  watch --> kick["wake the rebuild task"]
  kick --> render["re-render + execute cells"]
  render --> diff["diff vs the live block list"]
  diff --> ws["websocket: changed ops"]
  ws --> dom["browser swaps blocks in place"]
```

Saving `post.tmd` drives this loop end to end: the watcher wakes, the rebuild
re-renders and re-runs the changed cell, the diff finds the single changed block,
and the websocket carries one `update` to the browser.

On a websocket connect the server sends a `full_render` (and the page was already
server-rendered into `#tali-root` for the first paint). On each save it diffs and
broadcasts **only the ops** (`update`/`insert`/`remove`), or a fresh `full_render`
when recovering from a previous failure, plus a `diagnostics` message when those
change. The full message contract is in [the websocket protocol](protocol.tmd).

## What the target means (`serve_site::Target`)

`preview` takes either a directory or a `.tmd`, and both end up in the same server:

- a **directory** is the project;
- a **`.tmd`** is resolved to the project it belongs to — the nearest ancestor
  `_site.yml` — and the browser opens at that document's page;
- a `.tmd` with **no** ancestor `_site.yml` gets a project of exactly itself
  (`Site::discover_single`), rooted at its own directory.

The third case is scoped on purpose: previewing a scratch note must not pull thirty
unrelated siblings into the nav, nor parse them to find that out. The scope is carried
on `Project::scope`, so a later re-discovery (a `_site.yml` save, a new `.tmd`) cannot
silently widen it.

## The server (`serve_site/mod.rs`)

State is **per page**, built lazily on first visit:

```rust
struct SiteApp {
    site: Mutex<Site>,                       // the discovered project (config, pages, …)
    pages: Mutex<HashMap<String, PageState>>, // rel-path → { live blocks, broadcast tx }
    build_tx: UnboundedSender<BuildMsg>,     // → the executor worker
}
```

Navigation between pages is just a real page load (each page has its own
websocket and its own executor), so the navbar / prev-next links work with no SPA.
A save rebuilds only the affected page and hot-reloads it in place with the same
block diff; a `_site.yml` change re-discovers the whole site and reloads open
tabs. A single spawned **executor worker** drains `build_tx`, so each page's cells
run on its own kernel without blocking the HTTP handlers; `build_page_guarded`
gives it `catch_unwind` isolation. To keep a long
browse from leaking a kernel per visited page, the worker holds at most
`MAX_WARM_PAGES` (6) warm executors and evicts the least-recently-built (dropping its
executor kills its kernel children); an evicted page just pays a cold kernel start on
its next edit.

### Panic isolation

Builds run in a spawned task, so a panic in the render/exec path would silently kill
hot-reload for the rest of the session. `build_page_guarded` wraps each build in
`catch_unwind` (via `AssertUnwindSafe` over the awaited future, so the panic is caught
rather than propagated): on a caught panic it pulls a human message off the payload
(`panic_msg` downcasts the `Box<dyn Any>` to `&str`/`String`), logs it, and broadcasts
a `protocol::error` so every connected client shows an error overlay. The worker
survives, so the next good save re-renders and clears the overlay (the errored flag
makes that recovery a full re-mount even when the diff is empty). Because the state
lives behind a `parking_lot::Mutex` (not `std::sync::Mutex`), a panic while the lock is
held releases it cleanly rather than poisoning it.

### The watcher

`spawn_watcher` runs `notify` on its own thread (notify is synchronous) and watches the
root project **plus every mounted sub-project**, so an edit to a mounted page
hot-reloads exactly like a root one. Events are pumped through a channel so that thread
owns the watcher and can register watches for directories created after startup: the
recursive-watch model added an inotify descriptor per directory, including
`node_modules` and `.git`, which a large project uses to exhaust `max_user_watches` and
kill hot reload outright.

Skipping `_freeze` matters: the executor writes its own cache there on every run, and
without the skip each write would kick a redundant rebuild. The filter as a whole keeps
an unrelated save (an editor swap file, a build-output write) from triggering a wasteful
0-op rebuild. A burst of saves is debounced so several files coalesce into one rebuild.

## Why one server, not two

There used to be two: `serve/mod.rs` for a single document and `serve_site/mod.rs` for a
directory. The stated reason was simplicity — the single-doc path has no chrome, no
cross-page links and one block state, so keeping it separate kept each one small.

That reasoning did not survive contact with the result. "One document" is not a
different *kind* of thing from "a project"; it is a project with one page, and modelling
it as its own server bought a second copy of the watcher, the rebuild loop, the diff
broadcast and the page shell, which then drifted. The single-doc copy never gained the
warm-executor pool, so a document with no ancestor `_site.yml` cold-started its kernel
where a project preview did not. Worse, previewing a file *inside* a project served it
alone: no nav, no breadcrumb, every cross-page link dead — which is why the VS Code
companion had already stopped doing it and resolved to the project instead.

So the single-document server was folded away and its path kept as the shared layer.
The lesson is the general one about a second mechanism for one job: the duplicate does
not stay a simplification, it becomes the degraded copy, and the drift shows up as
behaviour the author has to remember rather than as a compile error.

## LAN exposure & the `--host` token

By default the server binds loopback only. `--host` binds `0.0.0.0` so a phone on
the same network can open the live preview (the console prints the LAN URL and a QR
code). That also means anyone on the network could reach it, so a shared guard in
`serve/security.rs` (`new_session_token` / `lan_access` / `lan_token_guard`, installed via
`with_lan_guard` on both servers) gates **non-loopback** requests behind a
per-session token: the token is a random UUID minted at startup, threaded into the
LAN URL/QR as `?t=…`, and the first tokened load sets a `tali_token` cookie so the
rest of the session (assets, the websocket upgrade, reloads) authenticates with no
client changes. Loopback peers are detected from `ConnectInfo` and exempted, so the
local workflow (and the editor companion) is unaffected; a non-loopback request with
no/wrong token gets a `403`. The websocket `Origin` check (`origin_allowed`) is an
orthogonal, always-on guard against cross-site driving of the control channel.
